A floating-point operand of '&&' or '||' is never compared against zero. The
truth test is emitted as an integer 'test %rax,%rax' after the operand has been
loaded into an SSE register, so the branch is decided by whatever was left in
%rax and every such expression answers as though the operand were true -
'and_dd(2.5, 0.0)' returns 1, and so does 'and_dd(0.0, 0.0)'. There is no
comisd/ucomisd anywhere in the generated function.

The fix belongs in the legacy backend (src/x86_64/codegen_x86_64.c), where the
truth test for a logical operand is generated, and is out of scope by decision:
the legacy pipeline is not being taught this.

Muted here alone. Under '-experimental' this fixture passes - see
docs/ir-codegen-design.md section 11 step 20 for what that took - so if it ever
reports as passing *here* too, confirm a comisd against zero in the
disassembly before deleting this marker: with the answer decided by a register
nobody set, garbage lining up favourably looks identical from the exit code.
